Ah, the classic 5.5-hour timezone offset! This happens because your Spring Boot application is likely running on a server (or in a Docker container) that defaults to the **UTC** timezone, while you need it to record time in **IST (UTC +5:30)**.
Because we were so focused on the database locks in the previous steps, you missed the timezone fix I provided earlier for your bulk worker.
Here are the **two exact things** you need to change to permanently kill this 5.5-hour bug.
### 1. Fix the Bulk Insert Time (VoucherAsyncWorker.java)
In your bulk worker, you are currently converting an IST time object into an Instant. When Java's JDBC driver sees an Instant, it converts it *back* to the server's default timezone (UTC) before saving it to Oracle!
**Open VoucherAsyncWorker.java and find this exact code inside processChunkRaw (around line 220):**
```java
// --- FIND THESE TWO LINES ---
java.time.ZonedDateTime istTime = java.time.ZonedDateTime.now(java.time.ZoneId.of("Asia/Kolkata"));
ps.setTimestamp(2, java.sql.Timestamp.from(istTime.toInstant()));         // REQ_DATE

```
**REPLACE them with this:**
```java
// --- REPLACE WITH THIS ---
// This forces the exact IST wall-clock time into the database without UTC conversion
java.time.LocalDateTime istTime = java.time.LocalDateTime.now(java.time.ZoneId.of("Asia/Kolkata"));
ps.setTimestamp(2, java.sql.Timestamp.valueOf(istTime));         // REQ_DATE

```
### 2. The "Silver Bullet" Global Timezone Fix
If your bulk rows are now fixing themselves, but you notice that your *Batch Creation Time* or *Workflow Approval Time* is still 5 hours behind, it is because you have LocalDateTime.now() scattered across your VoucherCommandService and VoucherWorkflowEngine.
Instead of hunting down every single LocalDateTime.now() and changing it, you can force your entire Spring Boot application to run in the Asia/Kolkata timezone the moment it starts up.
**Open your main application class** (the one with @SpringBootApplication, usually VoucherServiceApplication.java or similar) and add this method:
```java
import jakarta.annotation.PostConstruct;
import java.util.TimeZone;

@SpringBootApplication
public class VoucherServiceApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(VoucherServiceApplication.class, args);
    }

    // --- ADD THIS METHOD ---
    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        // Forces the entire Spring Boot app to use IST, completely bypassing server UTC defaults
        TimeZone.setDefault(TimeZone.getTimeZone("Asia/Kolkata"));
    }
}

```
### Why this solves it forever:
By applying the @PostConstruct fix, every time your code calls LocalDateTime.now()—whether it's generating a journal request date, logging a workflow action, or setting the batch execution time—it will automatically pull the correct local time, regardless of where your server is physically located.
Make those two changes, restart your server, and your 5.5-hour time travel bug will be gone for good!
